            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tBy 2000, Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 2016, bequest of Gillett G. Griffin to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] Included in an inventory of the Griffin collection created in 2000 (G1831).\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Beginning in the seventh century CE, technologies of smelting, cold-working, and lost-wax casting are thought to have been introduced to western Mexico by seafaring traders from the Pacific coastal cultures of Colombia and Ecuador. In West Mexico, the technology continued to be refined; relatively pure copper cold-work and casting were supplemented with the use of bronze—copper alloyed with tin or arsenic—which allowed for thinner, more intricate casting. The thin coils of wax that formed the original model, encased in a combination of ash and clay to make a mold, are retained in the surface pattern of the cast bells. The sound of these bells was of primary importance, although experimentation with color, by adding gold or silver to the alloys, suggests that visual qualities also mattered. Corrosion conceals the original colors of these bells, but they could be determined by scientific analysis.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T3G-7_CLA_FA.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
